.TH "SR" "1" "2026-10-12" "sr 7.0.0" "User Commands"
.ds Pg sr-search-replace
.hy 0
.SH NAME
sr \- safe, auditable search and replace for source trees
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
sr [GLOBAL OPTIONS] <command> [OPTIONS] [ARGS...]
sr [GLOBAL OPTIONS] [GLOB...] SEARCH REPLACE
.fi
.PP
A bare invocation (no known command as the first word) is a shortcut for sr replace. Flags may appear in any position.
.SH DESCRIPTION
.PP
sr rewrites text across a whole tree of files, and keeps an audit trail of every change so you can review and undo it.  It is meant for the jobs where `sed -i` is too blunt and an editor's project-wide replace is too manual:
.PP
.nf
  * renaming an API across a repository
  * bumping versions in dozens of manifests
  * migrating a config key or an import path
  * normalising whitespace, encoding or line endings
  * bulk renaming files
.fi
.PP
Design rules that follow from that:
.PP
.nf
  1. Literal by default.  A search string is a search string; \(ga.\(ga does not
     suddenly mean "any character".  Regular expressions are opt\-in.
  2. One engine.  Counting, previewing and replacing all use the same matcher,
     so the number sr reports is the number of edits it makes.
  3. Nothing is written before the whole tree is scanned.  The confirmation
     prompt and the summary therefore state real numbers, not estimates.
  4. Every mutating run is reversible.  Originals are stored content\-addressed
     under <target>/.sr with a manifest, a journal and a diff per file.
  5. Fail loudly, never silently.  Undecodable files are skipped and reported;
     binary files are skipped unless asked for; drift after a session is
     detected before a rollback overwrites anything.
  6. Machine friendly.  Stable exit codes, \-\-json, \-\-porcelain, \-\-yes and a
     lock so concurrent runs cannot interleave.
.fi
.SH QUICK START
.PP
1. Look before you leap:
.PP
.nf
     sr find "old_api" \-p \(aq*.py\(aq
     sr diff "old_api" "new_api" \-p \(aq*.py\(aq
.fi
.PP
.nf
   \(gafind\(ga shows file:line:col for every occurrence, \(gadiff\(ga shows the exact
   unified diff a replace would produce.  Neither writes anything.
.fi
.PP
2. Do it:
.PP
.nf
     sr replace "old_api" "new_api" \-p \(aq*.py\(aq
.fi
.PP
.nf
   The scan phase counts the changes first; if more than 50 files would change
   you are asked to confirm.  Originals are stored in a session under .sr/.
.fi
.PP
3. Check and, if needed, undo:
.PP
.nf
     git diff                       # or: sr diff \-\-session latest
     sr rollback                # restores every file of the last session
.fi
.PP
4. Useful variants:
.PP
.nf
     sr replace "old" "new" \-\-dry\-run          # count only
     sr replace "old" "new" \-\-interactive      # confirm every hunk
     sr replace "old" "new" \-\-write=dir \-o out # write copies, touch nothing
     sr replace "old" "new" \-\-json | jq .      # machine readable
     sr replace "old" "new" \-C ~/project       # another directory
.fi
.PP
5. Files are skipped for a reason.  Add -v to see why:
.PP
.nf
     sr replace "old" "new" \-v
.fi
.PP
.nf
   You will see "binary content", "ignored by .gitignore", "excluded
   directory (node_modules)" and so on.
.fi
.SH PATTERN MATCHING
.PP
Four pattern kinds, selected by a flag (literal is the default):
.PP
.nf
  (default)   literal text.  \(ga.\(ga is a dot, \(ga(\(ga is a parenthesis.
              Escape sequences \(rsn \(rst \(rsr \(rs0 \(rsxNN are interpreted, so
              \(gasr replace \(aqa\(rstb\(aq \(aqa b\(aq\(ga matches a real tab.
              The replacement is inserted verbatim — \(ga$1\(ga, \(ga\(rs1\(ga and \(ga&\(ga
              stay literal, which makes config files safe to edit.
              Use \-\-no\-escapes to disable escape processing.
.fi
.PP
.nf
  \-\-regex     Python regular expressions.  The replacement is a template:
              \(rs1, \(rsg<name>, \(rsn and \(rs\(rs work as in re.sub.
                sr replace \(aqversion = "(\(rsd+)"\(aq \(aqversion = "\(rs1.dev0"\(aq \-\-regex
.fi
.PP
.nf
  \-\-ere       POSIX Extended Regular Expressions (grep \-E / sed \-E syntax),
              translated to Python: [[:digit:]], \(rs<, \(rs> and {m,n} work.
              Useful for patterns copied from shell scripts.
                sr replace \(aq[[:digit:]]{4}\-[[:digit:]]{4}\(aq \(aqXXXX\-XXXX\(aq \-\-ere
.fi
.PP
.nf
  \-\-pcre      Perl syntax: \(rsA, \(rsz, (?<name>...), atomic groups and
              possessive quantifiers (Python 3.11+).
                sr replace \(aq(?<=user=)[^&]+\(aq \(aqadmin\(aq \-\-pcre
.fi
.PP
Modifiers (they apply to every kind):
.PP
.nf
  \-i, \-\-ignore\-case      case\-insensitive
  \-w, \-\-word\-boundary    whole words only (wraps the pattern in \(rsb)
  \-m, \-\-multiline        the pattern may span lines (\(aq.\(aq matches newline)
  \-L, \-\-line\-range R     restrict edits to lines R (10, 10\-20, 10\-, \-20)
  \-M, \-\-max\-count N      at most N replacements per file
  \-\-no\-global            only the first occurrence on each line
  \-\-context N            context lines in previews and diffs (default 3)
.fi
.PP
Counting rules
.PP
`matches` counts occurrences, not lines: a line with three hits counts three. That is what the summary reports and what --max-count limits.  Matches outside --line-range are reported separately and never replaced.
.PP
Because one engine does both the counting and the replacing, `-i` and `-w` cannot disagree between the two — a class of bug that made the reported number of replacements wrong in grep+sed based tools.
.PP
Escaping cheat sheet
.PP
.nf
  literal dot            sr replace "1.2.3" "1.2.4"
  regex dot              sr replace \(aq1\(rs.2\(rs.3\(aq \(aq1.2.4\(aq \-\-regex
  literal backslash      sr replace \(aqC:\(rs\(rstemp\(aq \(aqD:\(rs\(rsdata\(aq \-\-no\-escapes
  newline in the pattern sr replace \(aqa\(rsnb\(aq \(aqa b\(aq \-\-multiline
  tab                    sr replace \(aq\(rst\(aq \(aq    \(aq
  hex byte               sr replace \(aq\(rsx00\(aq \(aq\(aq \-\-binary
.fi
.SH TARGET SELECTION
.PP
The positional GLOB matches the file *name*:
.PP
.nf
     sr replace "a" "b" \(aq*.py\(aq          # every .py below the target
     sr replace "a" "b"                 # every text file (GLOB defaults to \(aq*\(aq)
.fi
.PP
A GLOB containing `/` matches the path relative to the target and supports `**`:
.PP
.nf
     sr replace "a" "b" \-\-include \(aqsrc/**/*.py\(aq
.fi
.PP
Selection flags:
.PP
.nf
  \-p, \-\-pattern GLOB     repeatable; several patterns are OR\-ed
  \-\-file PATH            explicit file, bypasses pattern matching (repeatable)
  \-\-files\-from FILE      read the file list from FILE (\(aq\-\(aq for stdin)
  \-\-include GLOB         additional include filter (repeatable)
  \-\-exclude GLOB         exclusion; wins over \-\-include and \-p (repeatable)
  \-\-exclude\-dir NAME     prune a directory by name (repeatable)
  \-C, \-\-target DIR       the root to operate on (default: cwd)
  \-\-no\-recursive         only the target directory itself
  \-\-max\-depth N          descend at most N levels
  \-\-hidden               include dotfiles and dot\-directories
  \-\-symlinks             follow symlinked files (skipped by default)
  \-\-gitignore            honour .gitignore and core.excludesFile
  \-\-max\-size SIZE        skip files larger than SIZE (default 100MB)
  \-\-min\-size SIZE        skip files smaller than SIZE
  \-\-newer\-than AGE       only files modified within AGE (24h, 7d, 30m)
  \-\-older\-than AGE       only files not modified within AGE
  \-\-binary               process files that look binary
  \-\-no\-default\-excludes  do not apply the built\-in noise lists
.fi
.PP
Defaults, and why
.PP
Excluded directories: .git .hg .svn node_modules vendor dist build target
.PP
.nf
  __pycache__ .venv .cache .gradle .terraform .idea .vscode …
.fi
.PP
Excluded file names: .DS_Store Thumbs.db *.orig *.rej *.bak *.backup *.swp *~ Hidden files: excluded (pass --hidden). Symlinks: skipped (pass --symlinks) so a link cannot cause the same file to be
.PP
.nf
  edited twice or escape the target tree.
.fi
.PP
Binary files: skipped by content sniffing (NUL byte or >30% non-text bytes in
.PP
.nf
  the first 8 KiB), not by extension alone.
.fi
.PP
.sr/ (the session store) is always pruned, which keeps `rollback` idempotent.
.PP
Feed the file list from another tool
.PP
.nf
     git ls\-files \(aq*.py\(aq | sr replace "old" "new" \-\-files\-from \-
     sr find "TODO" \-\-porcelain | cut \-d: \-f1 | sort \-u > files
     sr replace "old" "new" \-\-files\-from files
.fi
.PP
Why `--files-from -` is safer than a glob: the list is exactly what git tracks, so generated files, vendored copies and ignored paths cannot slip in.
.SH BACKUPS AND SESSIONS
.PP
Every mutating run creates a *session*:
.PP
.nf
  <target>/.sr/sessions/<id>/
    manifest.json    who, what, when; per\-file hashes, sizes, mode, uid/gid
    journal.jsonl    append\-only op log (survives a crash mid\-run)
    files/<xx>/<sha256>.dat   the original bytes, content addressed
    diffs/<path>.diff         the unified diff of the change
    .lock                     advisory lock, held for the duration of the run
.fi
.PP
Content addressing means identical originals are stored once, so a run that touches 10 000 files sharing the same header costs one blob.  Every blob is verified on read: a corrupt or truncated backup is reported instead of silently restoring garbage.
.PP
Where sessions live
.PP
Default: <target>/.sr.  Add `.sr/` to .gitignore. Elsewhere: --backup-dir ~/sr-backups/myproject (also useful when the target is read-only or when you do not want to touch the repository at all).
.PP
Retention
.PP
.nf
  \-\-keep N           keep the newest N sessions (default 20, 0 = never prune)
  \-\-keep\-age AGE     also drop sessions older than AGE (e.g. 30d)
  sr sessions size     how much disk the store uses
  sr sessions prune \-\-keep 10 \-\-keep\-age 30d
.fi
.PP
Pruning runs automatically at the end of a mutating run, so the store cannot grow without bound.
.PP
Disable backups
.PP
.nf
  \-\-no\-backup   faster and no .sr/ directory, but \(garollback\(ga will not work.
                The right choice inside a clean git working tree, where
                \(gagit checkout \-\- .\(ga is your rollback.
.fi
.PP
What is *not* in a session
.PP
Renames are journalled (as from/to pairs) but the file content is not copied again — the content is unchanged.  --write=dir and --write=stdout runs create no session because no original is modified.
.SH ROLLBACK
.PP
.nf
  sr rollback                        # newest session
  sr rollback latest
  sr rollback 9f3a1c                 # unique id suffix
  sr rollback 20260131T120530Z\-9f3a1c
  sr rollback 2                      # the second newest (ordinal)
  sr rollback latest \-\-dry\-run       # show what would be restored
  sr rollback latest \-\-file src/app.py
  sr rollback latest \-\-force \-\-yes   # unattended, overwrite drifted files
.fi
.PP
How a session is referenced
.PP
full id → unique suffix → ordinal (1 = newest) → `latest`.  Ambiguous suffixes are reported with the candidate list instead of guessing.
.PP
Drift detection
.PP
For every file the manifest stores the SHA-256 of the content *after* the run. Before restoring, sr hashes the current file:
.PP
.nf
  * unchanged  → restored silently
  * changed    → reported as drifted and SKIPPED, unless \-\-force
  * missing    → restored (the session recreates the directory)
.fi
.PP
That is what makes `rollback` safe to run hours later: it will not silently throw away edits you made in the meantime.
.PP
The rollback is itself reversible
.PP
Unless --no-safety-snapshot is given, sr records the pre-rollback content in a new session of kind `rollback`.  So:
.PP
.nf
  sr rollback                # oops, wrong session
  sr sessions                # the top entry is the safety snapshot
  sr rollback <snapshot\-id>  # back to the state before the rollback
.fi
.PP
Renames recorded in the session are undone in reverse order (deepest first), so renaming a directory back cannot be blocked by its own children.
.PP
Exit codes
.PP
.nf
  0  everything restored
  4  at least one file could not be restored (permissions, missing blob)
.fi
.PP
Rollback refuses to touch paths outside the session root, paths containing control characters, and anything under /proc, /sys or /dev.
.SH RENAMING FILES
.PP
.nf
  sr rename ".txt" ".md" \-p \(aq*.txt\(aq
  sr rename "IMG_" "photo\-" \-p \(aqIMG_*\(aq
  sr rename \-\-regex \(aq^(.*)\(rs.jpeg$\(aq \(aq\(rs1.jpg\(aq
  sr replace "old" "new" \-\-rename \-p \(aq*.py\(aq      # content *and* names
  sr rename "a" "b" \-\-dry\-run                    # plan only
.fi
.PP
Semantics
.PP
* The replacement is applied to the file *name* only, never to the directory
.PP
.nf
  part of the path, so a rename can never move a file to another directory.
.fi
.PP
* Directories are renamed too (deepest path first, so children keep valid
.PP
.nf
  paths).  With \(gasr replace \-\-rename\(ga directories are left alone unless
  \-\-directories is given, because renaming folders as a side effect of a
  content edit is surprising.
.fi
.PP
* New names containing a path separator are refused. * Collisions are detected before anything is executed: if two files would get
.PP
.nf
  the same new name, both are skipped and reported instead of one overwriting
  the other.  An existing target is also refused unless \-\-force.
.fi
.PP
* Renames are journalled, so `sr rollback` undoes them in reverse order.
.PP
Rename + content in one pass
.PP
.nf
  sr replace "MyClass" "YourClass" \-\-rename \-p \(aq*.py\(aq
.fi
.PP
The content pass runs first, then the rename pass, both inside one session — so a single `sr rollback` restores the old content *and* the old names.
.SH INTERACTIVE MODE
.PP
.nf
  sr replace "old" "new" \-\-interactive
  sr replace "old" "new" \-I \-p \(aq*.py\(aq \-\-context 5
.fi
.PP
For every file, changes are grouped into hunks (matches within --context lines of each other).  Each hunk is rendered as a diff and you answer:
.PP
.nf
  y / enter   apply this hunk
  n           skip it
  a           apply this hunk and every remaining one in this file
  d           show the diff again
  q           skip the rest of the file
.fi
.PP
Hunks you reject are not written; the file is rewritten once, with only the accepted hunks applied.  The session records the actual result, so `rollback` restores the true original.
.PP
Non-interactive contexts
.PP
When stdin is not a terminal, --interactive degrades to "skip the remaining hunks" with a warning rather than blocking forever — pass --yes to apply everything unattended.  `--yes` also answers the "N files will change" confirmation, which is what CI needs.
.PP
Related safety valves
.PP
.nf
  \-\-dry\-run            count and preview, write nothing
  \-\-diff               show the diff of every change
  \-\-confirm\-threshold N  ask before writing when > N files would change
  \-\-write=dir \-o out   write copies instead of touching the originals
.fi
.SH MACHINE\-READABLE OUTPUT
.PP
--json
.PP
.nf
  One JSON document on stdout.  Every diagnostic (progress, warnings, errors)
  goes to stderr, so \(gasr replace ... \-\-json | jq\(ga is always safe.
.fi
.PP
.nf
  {
    "version": "7.0.0",
    "dry_run": false,
    "root": "/home/me/project",
    "search": "old", "replacement": "new", "pattern_kind": "fixed",
    "duration_seconds": 0.412,
    "totals": {
      "scanned": 128, "changed": 7, "unchanged": 121, "skipped": 2,
      "failed": 0, "matches": 19, "replacements": 19, "renamed": 0,
      "bytes_read": 421887, "bytes_written": 20144
    },
    "session": { "id": "20260131T120530Z\-9f3a1c", "directory": "...", ... },
    "files": [
      { "path": "...", "rel_path": "src/app.py", "status": "changed",
         "matches": 3, "replacements": 3, "encoding": "utf\-8",
         "size_before": 4012, "size_after": 4015,
         "diff": { "added": 3, "removed": 3, "changed": 6 } }
    ],
    "errors": [], "warnings": [], "exit_code": 0
  }
.fi
.PP
.nf
  Add \-\-diff to include the patch text of every file as "patch".
.fi
.PP
--porcelain
.PP
.nf
  Stable \(gapath:line:col:kind:text\(ga records, one per line, designed to be cut
  and piped:
.fi
.PP
.nf
     sr find "old" \-\-porcelain | cut \-d: \-f1 | sort \-u
.fi
.PP
Exit codes (see `sr help exit-codes`)
.PP
.nf
  0 success / at least one match      3 no match
  1 runtime error                     4 rollback failed
  2 usage error                       5 update failed
                                      6 selftest failed
                                    130 interrupted
.fi
.PP
CI recipe
.PP
.nf
  set \-euo pipefail
  sr replace "$OLD" "$NEW" \-p \(aq*.py\(aq \-\-yes \-\-json \-\-no\-update\-check > sr.json
  jq \-e \(aq.totals.failed == 0\(aq sr.json
  jq \-r \(aq.files[].rel_path\(aq sr.json
.fi
.PP
The `--no-update-check` flag keeps the network out of CI, and `--yes` avoids a prompt that would otherwise hang the job (sr refuses to prompt when stdin is not a terminal and exits non-zero instead).
.SH CONFIGURATION FILES
.PP
Precedence, lowest to highest:
.PP
.nf
  1. built\-in defaults
  2. /etc/sr/sr.conf                     system wide
  3. $XDG_CONFIG_HOME/sr/sr.conf         user (default ~/.config/sr/sr.conf)
  4. <repo root>/.srrc, <target>/.srrc,  project (walked upwards, stops at
     <target>/.sr/sr.conf                the repository boundary)
  5. $SR_CONFIG                          an extra file
  6. \-\-config\-file FILE                  an extra file, explicitly
  7. SR_* environment variables
  8. \-\-config KEY=VALUE
  9. command line flags
.fi
.PP
Syntax — a small, forgiving INI dialect:
.PP
.nf
    # a comment
    ignore\-case = true          # applies to every command
    exclude = *.min.js, dist    # comma or whitespace separated list
.fi
.PP
.nf
    [replace]                   # applies to \(gasr replace\(ga only
    backup = false
    confirm\-threshold = 500
.fi
.PP
.nf
    [find]
    context = 1
.fi
.PP
Rules:
.PP
.nf
  * keys are the option names (\(aq\-\(aq and \(aq_\(aq are interchangeable)
  * \(gakey\(ga alone means \(gakey = true\(ga
  * booleans accept true/false/yes/no/on/off/1/0
  * sizes accept 512, 4KB, 10MB, 1.5GiB; durations accept 30s, 5m, 7d
  * repeating a key builds a list
  * an unknown key or an invalid value is a hard error with file:line —
    a silently ignored typo is exactly what a bulk\-edit tool must not have
.fi
.PP
Commands:
.PP
.nf
  sr config list \-\-show\-origin    every option, its value, where it came from
  sr config show confirm\-threshold
  sr config init                  write a documented template
  sr config init \-\-file .srrc     …into the project instead
  sr config path                  the files sr would read, in order
  sr config validate              parse every file and report errors
.fi
.PP
A project .srrc is worth committing: it makes the team's exclusion list, confirmation threshold and backup policy part of the repository instead of tribal knowledge.
.SH SELF\-UPDATE
.PP
.nf
  sr update \-\-check          is there a newer release?
  sr update                  install it
  sr update \-\-version 7.1.0  install a specific version
  sr update \-\-channel prerelease
  sr update \-\-source npm     ask the npm registry instead of GitHub
  sr update \-\-repo owner/name \-\-path /usr/local/bin/sr
.fi
.PP
What sr detects
.PP
`sr doctor` prints the installation type.  It decides how an update is applied:
.PP
.nf
  standalone   a single executable file (zipapp or installed script).  sr can
               update itself: download → verify → smoke test → replace.
  pip / pipx   the environment owns the files, so sr prints the exact upgrade
               command instead of touching site\-packages.
  npm          prints \(ganpm install \-g sr\-search\-replace@latest\(ga.
  source       a git checkout — updating is refused, use \(gagit pull && make\(ga.
.fi
.PP
The update sequence for a standalone install
.PP
.nf
  1. ask the GitHub releases API for the newest release on the channel
     (SR_GITHUB_TOKEN/GITHUB_TOKEN raises the rate limit)
  2. compare versions with semver rules; stop if not newer (unless \-\-version)
  3. download the \(gasr.pyz\(ga asset to a temporary directory
  4. verify its SHA\-256 against the published \(gasr.pyz.sha256\(ga
     — a mismatch aborts the update and installs nothing
  5. smoke test: run \(gapython3 <downloaded> \-\-version\(ga in a subprocess
  6. copy the current file to \(ga<file>.bak\(ga
  7. atomically replace the file (write sibling + rename) and keep its mode
.fi
.PP
Every step either succeeds or leaves the current installation untouched.
.PP
Background check
.PP
At most once per 24h, sr looks for a newer release on a daemon thread with a 2.5s budget and caches the answer under $XDG_CACHE_HOME/sr/update-check.json. It never blocks a run: if the network is slow or absent, nothing is printed and the cache is used next time.  Turn it off with --no-update-check or SR_NO_UPDATE_CHECK=1 (a good idea in CI and on air-gapped machines).
.PP
Pinning
.PP
Install a specific version and disable the check to pin:
.PP
.nf
     sr update \-\-version 7.0.0
     export SR_NO_UPDATE_CHECK=1
.fi
.PP
Offline / air-gapped
.PP
Download the two release assets on a connected machine, copy them over, then:
.PP
.nf
     sha256sum \-c sr.pyz.sha256
     install \-m 0755 sr.pyz /usr/local/bin/sr
.fi
.SH PERFORMANCE
.PP
Where the time goes, in order of impact:
.PP
.nf
  1. Discovery.  Pruning is the whole game: sr prunes excluded directories
     *before* descending, so a tree with node_modules costs nothing.  Narrow
     the target with \-C and \-p instead of scanning everything.
  2. Reading and decoding files.  Every candidate is fully read once.  Use
     \-\-max\-size, \-\-min\-size, \-\-newer\-than and \-\-include to cut the set.
  3. Matching.  A literal pattern is compiled to a regex once and reused for
     every file, so per\-file cost is one search over the buffer.
  4. Writing.  Each changed file is written once, with fsync of the file and
     (for atomic writes) of the directory.
.fi
.PP
Practical numbers
.PP
On a laptop SSD, a literal replacement over ~10 000 text files (≈120 MB) takes a few seconds.  The scan phase doubles the read cost of a mutating run — that is the price of an accurate confirmation prompt and summary, and it is what makes `--dry-run` free of side effects.
.PP
Tips
.PP
.nf
  * Prefer \-\-files\-from with a list from git/ripgrep over scanning the tree.
  * Use \-\-no\-progress and \-q in scripts; the progress bar is a TTY\-only cost.
  * \-\-no\-backup skips the blob store entirely (use it inside a clean git tree).
  * \-\-keep 5 keeps the session store small on long\-lived projects.
  * Split huge runs per directory: sessions are per\-target, so a rollback then
    affects only that directory.
  * \-\-no\-lock removes one lock file per run; only relevant for thousands of
    tiny invocations.
.fi
.PP
What sr deliberately does not do
.PP
No parallelism by default.  A bulk rewrite is I/O bound, and parallel writers make the ordering of the journal (and therefore rollback) harder to reason about.  If you need throughput, shard by directory with -C and run the shards yourself — each shard gets its own session and can be rolled back independently.
.SH SECURITY MODEL
.PP
Threat model: sr runs with your own privileges over your own files, so the risks are accidental destruction, unintended scope, and supply-chain attacks on the update path — not remote exploitation.
.PP
Accidental destruction
.PP
.nf
  * literal matching by default: a pattern is never silently a regex
  * binary files are skipped unless \-\-binary is given
  * undecodable files are skipped and reported, never rewritten with a guessed
    encoding
  * the scan phase completes before the first write, so the confirmation prompt
    states real numbers
  * a confirmation prompt above \-\-confirm\-threshold (default 50 files)
  * VCS directories, build output and .sr/ are pruned by default
  * symlinks are skipped by default, so a link cannot smuggle a file outside
    the target tree into the edit set
  * running as root is refused unless \-\-allow\-root
  * rewriting / , /etc, /usr or $HOME directly is refused unless \-\-force
  * every mutating run is journalled and reversible; rollbacks are reversible
    too (safety snapshot)
  * rollback refuses to overwrite files that drifted after the session
.fi
.PP
Unintended scope
.PP
.nf
  * \-v prints every skipped candidate with the reason, so an empty result is
    explainable rather than mysterious
  * \-\-dry\-run and \-\-diff make the blast radius visible before committing
  * \-\-write=dir writes copies and touches nothing
.fi
.PP
Update path (supply chain)
.PP
.nf
  * HTTPS only; no plaintext fallback
  * the downloaded asset\(aqs SHA\-256 is verified against the published
    sr.pyz.sha256; a mismatch aborts and installs nothing
  * the download is smoke\-tested in a subprocess before it replaces anything
  * the previous file is kept as <file>.bak
  * \-\-no\-verify exists for mirrors without checksums and warns loudly
  * the background check is read\-only, cached, non\-blocking and can be disabled
    entirely (SR_NO_UPDATE_CHECK=1)
.fi
.PP
Data handling
.PP
.nf
  * file content never leaves the machine; the only network calls are the
    release lookup and the release download
  * sessions store original bytes under <target>/.sr — treat that directory
    with the same care as the files themselves (it is not encrypted), and add
    it to .gitignore so backups are never committed
  * no telemetry, no analytics, no crash reporting: a crash prints a traceback
    locally and suggests opening an issue
.fi
.PP
Reporting a vulnerability
.PP
.nf
  See SECURITY.md.  Please do not open a public issue for something that could
  destroy data; use the contact address listed there.
.fi
.SH COMPATIBILITY AND MIGRATION
.PP
Python
.PP
.nf
  Requires 3.10+.  Developed and CI\-tested on 3.10, 3.11, 3.12, 3.13 and 3.14.
  No third\-party runtime dependencies.
.fi
.PP
Operating systems
.PP
.nf
  Linux and macOS are supported.  Windows works under WSL2; native Windows is
  not supported because sr relies on POSIX rename semantics, \(gaos.chown\(ga,
  advisory locks and inode preservation.
.fi
.PP
Relationship to sr 6.x (the bash implementation)
.PP
sr 7 is a rewrite in Python with a redesigned command line.  The 6.x flags are not accepted.  Migration table:
.PP
.nf
  6.x                              7.x
  \-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-   \-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-
  sr "*.py" "a" "b"                sr replace "a" "b" \-p \(aq*.py\(aq
  sr \-i / \-\-ignore\-case            sr replace \-i …            (unchanged)
  sr \-E / \-\-extended\-regex         sr replace \-\-ere …
  sr \-w / \-\-word\-boundary          sr replace \-w …            (unchanged)
  sr \-m / \-\-multiline              sr replace \-m …            (unchanged)
  sr \-nr / \-\-no\-recursive          sr replace \-\-no\-recursive
  sr \-nb / \-\-no\-backup             sr replace \-\-no\-backup
  sr \-nbf / \-\-no\-backup\-folder     sr replace \-\-backup\-dir <dir>
  sr \-fb / \-\-force\-backup          (backups are on by default)
  sr \-xs / \-\-max\-size MB           sr replace \-\-max\-size 10MB
  sr \-xp / \-\-exclude\-patterns      sr replace \-\-exclude GLOB (repeatable)
  sr \-xd / \-\-exclude\-dirs          sr replace \-\-exclude\-dir NAME (repeatable)
  sr \-sd / \-\-search\-dir DIR        sr replace \-C DIR
  sr \-od / \-\-output\-dir DIR        sr replace \-\-write=dir \-o DIR
  sr \-mode copy                    sr replace \-\-write=dir \-o DIR
  sr \-mode backup_only             (covered by \-\-dry\-run + sessions)
  sr \-\-dry\-run                     sr replace \-\-dry\-run       (unchanged)
  sr \-\-rollback                    sr rollback
  sr \-\-rollback=DIR                sr rollback <session\-id>
  sr \-\-rollback\-list               sr sessions
  sr \-\-binary                      sr replace \-\-binary        (unchanged)
  sr \-\-binary\-method=…             (single robust sniffer; no knob)
  sr \-\-find\-opts/\-\-sed\-opts/…      removed: flags were injected into a shell
                                   command line, which was unsafe.  Use the
                                   typed equivalents (\-\-include, \-\-max\-depth,
                                   \-\-newer\-than, …).
  sr \-\-no\-global                   sr replace \-\-no\-global     (unchanged)
  sr \-\-delimiter CHAR              removed: no shell quoting is involved, so
                                   no delimiter can collide any more.
  SR_* environment variables       SR_* still exist, one per option, but the
                                   names follow the new option names.
.fi
.PP
Behavioural changes worth knowing
.PP
.nf
  * Patterns are literal by default.  6.x escaped the search string for sed,
    which also meant \(ga\-E\(ga was effectively broken; 7.x separates literal and
    regex cleanly.
  * Replacement counts are occurrences, not matching lines.
  * \(ga\-\-dry\-run\(ga writes nothing at all (6.x created a backup directory).
  * Sessions live in \(ga.sr/\(ga instead of \(gasr.backup.<timestamp>/\(ga in the project
    root, and old 6.x backup directories are not read.
  * Exit codes are documented and stable; "no match" is 3, not 1.
.fi
.PP
6.x sessions
.PP
`sr.backup.*` directories from 6.x are left untouched.  Restore from them with the 6.x script (still downloadable from the v6.1.0 release assets) or copy the files back by hand — the layout is a plain directory mirror.
.SH TROUBLESHOOTING
.PP
Start here: `sr doctor` checks the interpreter, the installation type, the config files, the session store, filesystem capabilities (atomic rename, inode preservation, symlinks, permissions, unicode names), locking, disk space and the network.  Then `sr selftest` proves the whole pipeline works end to end.
.PP
"no files matched the target selection"
.PP
.nf
  * Is the glob quoted?  \(gasr replace a b *.py\(ga lets the *shell* expand
    \(ga*.py\(ga; quote it: \(ga\(aq*.py\(aq\(ga.
  * Are the files hidden or inside an excluded directory?  Add \-v to see every
    skipped candidate and the reason.
  * Is \-\-gitignore hiding them?  Check with \(gasr find x \-\-gitignore \-v\(ga.
  * Wrong root?  \(ga\-C <dir>\(ga instead of relying on the cwd.
.fi
.PP
"skipped: binary content"
.PP
.nf
  sr sniffs the first 8 KiB: a NUL byte or >30% non\-text bytes means binary.
  UTF\-16 files without a BOM look binary.  Fix with \-\-encoding utf\-16 (then the
  file is decoded, not sniffed) or \-\-binary (forces processing).
.fi
.PP
"cannot decode as utf-8"
.PP
.nf
  The file is not UTF\-8.  Use \-\-encoding cp1251 (or whatever it really is), or
  \-\-encoding\-error replace to force a lossy decode.  sr never guesses silently:
  a wrong guess would corrupt the file.
.fi
.PP
Line endings changed
.PP
.nf
  They are preserved by default (per file, LF/CRLF/CR).  \-\-line\-endings lf
  normalises on purpose.  Mixed endings inside one file keep the dominant one.
.fi
.PP
Rollback says "file changed after the session"
.PP
.nf
  Correct behaviour: the file was edited after the session was recorded, so
  restoring would throw those edits away.  Review with
  \(gasr diff \-\-session <id>\(ga, then either accept the loss with \-\-force or
  restore the single file with \-\-file <path> \-\-force.
.fi
.PP
"another sr process is using <session>"
.PP
.nf
  A lock file is held.  If no sr is running, the previous run was killed;
  sr breaks locks whose owner process is gone, otherwise delete
  \(ga.sr/sessions/<id>/.lock\(ga.
.fi
.PP
Everything is slower than expected
.PP
.nf
  * \-v shows the discovery statistics (candidates, filtered, directories, time)
  * check that node_modules/dist/vendor are being pruned
  * \(ga\-\-no\-backup\(ga removes the blob writes if you have git as a safety net
.fi
.PP
sr crashed
.PP
.nf
  Re\-run with SR_DEBUG=1 for a traceback and open an issue with the output of
  \(gasr doctor \-\-json\(ga attached.
.fi
.PP
Getting help
.PP
.nf
  sr help topics        the manual\(aqs index
  sr help \-\-<option>    one option in depth, with an example
  sr <command> \-\-help   one command in full
.fi
.SH EXAMPLES
.PP
Basics
.PP
.nf
  sr replace "old_api" "new_api" \-p \(aq*.py\(aq
  sr replace "old" "new"                        # every text file below cwd
  sr replace "old" "new" \-C ~/projects/api      # another directory
  sr replace "old" "new" \-\-file src/a.py \-\-file src/b.py
  git ls\-files \(aq*.py\(aq | sr replace "old" "new" \-\-files\-from \-
.fi
.PP
Looking before leaping
.PP
.nf
  sr find "TODO" \-p \(aq*.py\(aq
  sr find "TODO" \-\-count
  sr find "TODO" \-l                             # file names only
  sr find "TODO" \-A 2 \-B 2                      # with context
  sr diff "old" "new" \-p \(aq*.md\(aq
  sr replace "old" "new" \-\-dry\-run
  sr replace "old" "new" \-\-dry\-run \-\-json | jq \(aq.totals\(aq
.fi
.PP
Regular expressions
.PP
.nf
  sr replace \(aqversion = "(\(rsd+\(rs.\(rsd+)"\(aq \(aqversion = "\(rs1.post0"\(aq \-\-regex \-p \(aqsetup.py\(aq
  sr replace \(aqimport old\(rsb\(aq \(aqimport new\(aq \-\-regex \-p \(aq*.py\(aq
  sr replace \(aq[[:digit:]]{3}\-[[:digit:]]{4}\(aq \(aqXXX\-XXXX\(aq \-\-ere
  sr replace \(aq(?<=password=)\(rsS+\(aq \(aqchangeme\(aq \-\-pcre \-p \(aq*.env\(aq \-\-hidden
  sr replace "cat" "dog" \-w                     # whole words only
  sr replace "Warning" "Notice" \-i
.fi
.PP
Scoped edits
.PP
.nf
  sr replace "old" "new" \-\-line\-range 1\-30 \-p \(aqheader*\(aq
  sr replace "TODO" "DONE" \-\-max\-count 1
  sr replace "a" "b" \-\-no\-global                # first per line
  sr replace "a" "b" \-\-include \(aqsrc/**/*.py\(aq \-\-exclude \(aqsrc/vendor/*\(aq
  sr replace "a" "b" \-\-max\-depth 2 \-\-hidden
  sr replace "a" "b" \-\-newer\-than 7d
.fi
.PP
Safety and review
.PP
.nf
  sr replace "old" "new" \-\-interactive
  sr replace "old" "new" \-\-write=dir \-\-output\-dir out
  sr replace "old" "new" \-\-confirm\-threshold 5
  sr sessions
  sr diff \-\-session latest
  sr rollback latest \-\-dry\-run
  sr rollback 9f3a1c \-\-file src/app.py
  sr sessions prune \-\-keep 10 \-\-keep\-age 30d
.fi
.PP
Renaming
.PP
.nf
  sr rename ".txt" ".md" \-p \(aq*.txt\(aq
  sr rename \-\-regex \(aq^(.*)\(rs.jpeg$\(aq \(aq\(rs1.jpg\(aq
  sr replace "MyClass" "YourClass" \-\-rename \-p \(aq*.py\(aq
.fi
.PP
Encodings and line endings
.PP
.nf
  sr replace "old" "new" \-\-encoding cp1251 \-p \(aq*.txt\(aq
  sr replace "old" "new" \-\-encoding utf\-16 \-\-hidden
  sr replace "\(rsr\(rsn" "\(rsn" \-\-multiline \-p \(aq*.sh\(aq   # CRLF → LF
  sr replace "old" "new" \-\-line\-endings lf \-p \(aq*.py\(aq
.fi
.PP
Automation
.PP
.nf
  sr replace "old" "new" \-\-yes \-\-json \-\-no\-update\-check > report.json
  sr find "old" \-\-porcelain | cut \-d: \-f1 | sort \-u
  sr replace "1.2.3" "1.2.4" \-p \(aqpackage.json\(aq \-p \(aqpyproject.toml\(aq \-\-no\-backup
  SR_IGNORE_CASE=true sr replace "warning" "notice" \-p \(aq*.md\(aq
.fi
.PP
Real-world migrations
.PP
.nf
  # bump a version everywhere it is declared
  sr replace \(aq"version": "6.1.0"\(aq \(aq"version": "7.0.0"\(aq \-p package.json
  # move an import path across a monorepo
  sr replace "from app.utils import" "from core.utils import" \-\-gitignore
  # retire a deprecated API, then review what is left
  sr replace "legacy_call(" "modern_call(" \-p \(aq*.py\(aq && sr find "legacy_call"
.fi
.SH COMMANDS
.TP
.B replace (aliases: r)
Search and replace text in files.
.br
Usage: sr replace [GLOB...] SEARCH REPLACE [options]
.TP
.B find (aliases: search, grep)
Show matches without changing anything.
.br
Usage: sr find [GLOB...] SEARCH [options]
.TP
.B diff
Preview changes as a unified diff.
.br
Usage: sr diff [GLOB...] SEARCH REPLACE | \-\-session ID [options]
.TP
.B rename (aliases: mv)
Rename files and directories by pattern.
.br
Usage: sr rename [GLOB...] SEARCH REPLACE [options]
.TP
.B rollback (aliases: undo, restore)
Restore files from a session backup.
.br
Usage: sr rollback [SESSION] [\-\-file PATH] [\-\-dry\-run] [\-\-force]
.TP
.B sessions (aliases: backups)
List, inspect and prune sessions.
.br
Usage: sr sessions [list|show|delete|prune|size] [SESSION]
.TP
.B update (aliases: upgrade)
Check for and install a newer sr release.
.br
Usage: sr update [\-\-check] [\-\-version X] [\-\-channel stable|prerelease]
.TP
.B doctor (aliases: diagnose)
Check the installation and environment.
.br
Usage: sr doctor [\-\-json] [\-\-offline]
.TP
.B completions
Print shell completion scripts.
.br
Usage: sr completions [bash|zsh|fish] [\-\-install]
.TP
.B config
Inspect and manage configuration files.
.br
Usage: sr config [list|show|init|path|validate] [KEY]
.TP
.B selftest (aliases: smoke)
Run the built\-in smoke test.
.br
Usage: sr selftest [\-\-quick] [\-\-json]
.TP
.B help
Show the help system (topics, options, examples).
.br
Usage: sr help [TOPIC|COMMAND|\-\-OPTION]
.SH OPTIONS
Options are declared once in the source and drive the parser, the
configuration files, the shell completions and this page.
.SS Target Selection
.TP
.B \-C, \-\-target DIR
Directory to operate on (default: the current directory). Default: ..
.br
Applies to: replace, find, diff, rename. Config key: target. Env: SR_TARGET.
.TP
.B \-p, \-\-pattern GLOB
Only files whose name matches GLOB (repeatable). Default: every text file.
.br
Applies to: replace, find, diff, rename. Config key: pattern. Env: SR_PATTERN.
.TP
.B \-\-file PATH
Explicit file to process, bypassing pattern matching (repeatable).
.br
Applies to: replace, find, diff, rename. Config key: file. Env: SR_FILE.
.TP
.B \-\-files\-from FILE
Read the list of target files from FILE, one per line (\(aq\-\(aq for stdin).
.br
Applies to: replace, find, diff, rename. Config key: files_from. Env: SR_FILES_FROM.
.TP
.B \-\-include GLOB
Additional include glob, may contain \(aq/\(aq and \(aq**\(aq (repeatable).
.br
Applies to: replace, find, diff, rename. Config key: include. Env: SR_INCLUDE.
.TP
.B \-\-exclude GLOB
Exclude glob; wins over \-\-include and \-\-pattern (repeatable).
.br
Applies to: replace, find, diff, rename. Config key: exclude. Env: SR_EXCLUDE.
.TP
.B \-\-exclude\-dir NAME
Prune a directory by name (repeatable; adds to the built\-in list).
.br
Applies to: replace, find, diff, rename. Config key: exclude_dir. Env: SR_EXCLUDE_DIR.
.TP
.B \-\-no\-default\-excludes
Do not apply the built\-in exclude lists (node_modules, dist, *.bak …).
.br
Applies to: replace, find, diff, rename. Config key: no_default_excludes. Env: SR_NO_DEFAULT_EXCLUDES.
.TP
.B \-1, \-\-no\-recursive
Only the target directory itself, no subdirectories.
.br
Applies to: replace, find, diff, rename. Config key: no_recursive. Env: SR_NO_RECURSIVE.
.TP
.B \-\-max\-depth N
Descend at most N levels below the target directory.
.br
Applies to: replace, find, diff, rename. Config key: max_depth. Env: SR_MAX_DEPTH.
.TP
.B \-\-hidden
Also process dotfiles and dot\-directories.
.br
Applies to: replace, find, diff, rename. Config key: hidden. Env: SR_HIDDEN.
.TP
.B \-\-symlinks
Follow symlinked files (they are skipped by default).
.br
Applies to: replace, find, diff, rename. Config key: symlinks. Env: SR_SYMLINKS.
.TP
.B \-\-gitignore
Honour .gitignore files (including the global excludesfile).
.br
Applies to: replace, find, diff, rename. Config key: gitignore. Env: SR_GITIGNORE.
.TP
.B \-\-max\-size SIZE
Skip files larger than SIZE (default 100MB; a bare number means megabytes). Default: 104857600.
.br
Applies to: replace, find, diff, rename. Config key: max_size. Env: SR_MAX_SIZE.
.TP
.B \-\-min\-size SIZE
Skip files smaller than SIZE.
.br
Applies to: replace, find, diff, rename. Config key: min_size. Env: SR_MIN_SIZE.
.TP
.B \-\-newer\-than AGE
Only files modified within AGE (e.g. 24h, 7d, 30m).
.br
Applies to: replace, find, diff, rename. Config key: newer_than. Env: SR_NEWER_THAN.
.TP
.B \-\-older\-than AGE
Only files not modified within AGE.
.br
Applies to: replace, find, diff, rename. Config key: older_than. Env: SR_OLDER_THAN.
.TP
.B \-\-binary
Process files that look binary instead of skipping them.
.br
Applies to: replace, find, diff, rename. Config key: binary. Env: SR_BINARY.
.SS Matching
.TP
.B \-R, \-\-regex
Treat PATTERN as a Python regular expression.
.br
Applies to: replace, find, diff, rename. Config key: regex. Env: SR_REGEX.
.TP
.B \-\-ere
Treat PATTERN as a POSIX Extended Regular Expression (grep \-E syntax).
.br
Applies to: replace, find, diff, rename. Config key: ere. Env: SR_ERE.
.TP
.B \-\-pcre
Treat PATTERN as a Perl\-compatible regular expression.
.br
Applies to: replace, find, diff, rename. Config key: pcre. Env: SR_PCRE.
.TP
.B \-\-pattern\-kind KIND
Explicit pattern kind: fixed, regex, ere or pcre. One of: fixed, regex, ere, pcre. Default: fixed.
.br
Applies to: replace, find, diff, rename. Config key: pattern_kind. Env: SR_PATTERN_KIND.
.TP
.B \-i, \-\-ignore\-case
Case\-insensitive matching.
.br
Applies to: replace, find, diff, rename. Config key: ignore_case. Env: SR_IGNORE_CASE.
.TP
.B \-w, \-\-word\-boundary
Match whole words only.
.br
Applies to: replace, find, diff, rename. Config key: word_boundary. Env: SR_WORD_BOUNDARY.
.TP
.B \-m, \-\-multiline
Allow the pattern to span line boundaries (\(aq.\(aq matches newline).
.br
Applies to: replace, find, diff, rename. Config key: multiline. Env: SR_MULTILINE.
.TP
.B \-\-no\-escapes
Do not interpret \(rsn, \(rst, \(rsxNN in fixed patterns/replacements.
.br
Applies to: replace, find, diff, rename. Config key: no_escapes. Env: SR_NO_ESCAPES.
.TP
.B \-L, \-\-line\-range RANGE
Restrict edits to a line range: 10, 10\-20, 10\- or \-20.
.br
Applies to: replace, find, diff, rename. Config key: line_range. Env: SR_LINE_RANGE.
.TP
.B \-M, \-\-max\-count N
Replace at most N occurrences per file.
.br
Applies to: replace, find, diff, rename. Config key: max_count. Env: SR_MAX_COUNT.
.TP
.B \-\-no\-global
Replace only the first occurrence on each line.
.br
Applies to: replace, find, diff, rename. Config key: no_global. Env: SR_NO_GLOBAL.
.TP
.B \-\-context N
Lines of context around a match in preview/diff output (default 3). Default: 3.
.br
Applies to: replace, find, diff, rename. Config key: context. Env: SR_CONTEXT.
.SS Action
.TP
.B \-n, \-\-dry\-run
Report what would change; never write, never create a session.
.br
Applies to: replace, find, diff, rename. Config key: dry_run. Env: SR_DRY_RUN.
.TP
.B \-\-diff
Show a unified diff of every change (implies a preview).
.br
Applies to: replace, find, diff, rename. Config key: diff. Env: SR_DIFF.
.TP
.B \-I, \-\-interactive
Confirm every hunk before it is written.
.br
Applies to: replace, find, diff, rename. Config key: interactive. Env: SR_INTERACTIVE.
.TP
.B \-\-write MODE
Where the result goes: in\-place (default), stdout, or \-\-output\-dir. One of: in\-place, stdout, dir. Default: in\-place.
.br
Applies to: replace. Config key: write. Env: SR_WRITE.
.TP
.B \-o, \-\-output\-dir DIR
Write modified copies under DIR, mirroring the tree (needs \-\-write=dir).
.br
Applies to: replace. Config key: output_dir. Env: SR_OUTPUT_DIR.
.TP
.B \-\-suffix EXT
When writing copies, append EXT to the file name instead of mirroring.
.br
Applies to: replace. Config key: suffix. Env: SR_SUFFIX.
.TP
.B \-\-rename
Also apply the replacement to file and directory names.
.br
Applies to: replace. Config key: rename. Env: SR_RENAME.
.TP
.B \-\-rename\-only
Rename matching files/directories without touching their content.
.br
Applies to: replace. Config key: rename_only. Env: SR_RENAME_ONLY.
.TP
.B \-E, \-\-encoding NAME
Force an encoding (utf\-8, cp1251, latin\-1 …); default is auto\-detect.
.br
Applies to: replace, find, diff, rename. Config key: encoding. Env: SR_ENCODING.
.TP
.B \-\-encoding\-error POLICY
What to do with undecodable bytes: strict (skip), replace, ignore. One of: strict, replace, ignore. Default: strict.
.br
Applies to: replace, find, diff, rename. Config key: encoding_error. Env: SR_ENCODING_ERROR.
.TP
.B \-\-line\-endings MODE
Keep the file\(aqs line endings (default) or normalise to LF/CRLF. One of: preserve, lf, crlf. Default: preserve.
.br
Applies to: replace. Config key: line_endings. Env: SR_LINE_ENDINGS.
.TP
.B \-\-preserve\-mtime
Restore the original modification time after writing.
.br
Applies to: replace. Config key: preserve_mtime. Env: SR_PRESERVE_MTIME.
.TP
.B \-y, \-\-yes
Answer \(aqyes\(aq to every prompt; required for unattended runs in CI.
.br
Applies to: replace, rename, rollback, sessions, update. Config key: yes. Env: SR_YES.
.SS Safety & Backups
.TP
.B \-\-backup, \-\-no\-backup
Store originals in a session so the run can be rolled back (default on). Default: True.
.br
Applies to: replace, rename. Config key: backup. Env: SR_BACKUP.
.TP
.B \-\-backup\-dir DIR
Where to keep sessions (default <target>/.sr). Use an absolute path to keep them out of the tree.
.br
Applies to: replace, rename. Config key: backup_dir. Env: SR_BACKUP_DIR.
.TP
.B \-\-keep N
Keep at most N sessions; older ones are pruned after a run (default 20, 0 disables pruning). Default: 20.
.br
Applies to: replace, rename, sessions. Config key: keep. Env: SR_KEEP.
.TP
.B \-\-keep\-age AGE
Also drop sessions older than AGE (e.g. 30d).
.br
Applies to: replace, rename, sessions. Config key: keep_age. Env: SR_KEEP_AGE.
.TP
.B \-\-atomic, \-\-no\-atomic
Write via a temporary file + rename (default) or rewrite the inode in place. Default: True.
.br
Applies to: replace. Config key: atomic. Env: SR_ATOMIC.
.TP
.B \-\-lock, \-\-no\-lock
Take an advisory lock so two sr runs cannot interleave (default on). Default: True.
.br
Applies to: replace, rename. Config key: lock. Env: SR_LOCK.
.TP
.B \-\-confirm\-threshold N
Ask for confirmation when more than N files would change (0 disables). Default: 50.
.br
Applies to: replace, rename. Config key: confirm_threshold. Env: SR_CONFIRM_THRESHOLD.
.TP
.B \-\-allow\-root
Permit a mutating run with euid 0 (refused by default).
.br
Applies to: replace, rename. Config key: allow_root. Env: SR_ALLOW_ROOT.
.TP
.B \-f, \-\-force
Override safety guards (drifted files, read\-only warnings, existing targets).
.br
Applies to: replace, rename, rollback. Config key: force. Env: SR_FORCE.
.SS Output & Reporting
.TP
.B \-v, \-\-verbose
Show per\-file results and the reason skipped files were skipped.
.br
Applies to: every command. Config key: verbose. Env: SR_VERBOSE.
.TP
.B \-q, \-\-quiet
Only errors; suitable for scripts.
.br
Applies to: every command. Config key: quiet. Env: SR_QUIET.
.TP
.B \-\-verbosity LEVEL
Set the log level explicitly (overrides \-v/\-q). One of: quiet, normal, verbose, debug. Default: normal.
.br
Applies to: every command. Config key: verbosity. Env: SR_VERBOSITY.
.TP
.B \-\-color, \-\-no\-color WHEN
Colourise output. Honours NO_COLOR and CLICOLOR_FORCE. One of: auto, always, never. Default: auto.
.br
Applies to: every command. Config key: color. Env: SR_COLOR.
.TP
.B \-\-json
Emit a single JSON document on stdout; diagnostics go to stderr.
.br
Applies to: every command. Config key: json. Env: SR_JSON.
.TP
.B \-\-porcelain
Emit stable \(aqpath:line:col:text\(aq records, one per match.
.br
Applies to: replace, find, diff, rename. Config key: porcelain. Env: SR_PORCELAIN.
.TP
.B \-\-progress, \-\-no\-progress
Show a progress bar on a terminal (default on). Default: True.
.br
Applies to: replace, find, diff, rename. Config key: progress. Env: SR_PROGRESS.
.TP
.B \-\-summary, \-\-no\-summary
Print the run summary (default on). Default: True.
.br
Applies to: replace, find, diff, rename. Config key: summary. Env: SR_SUMMARY.
.SS Global (before or after the subcommand)
.TP
.B \-h, \-\-help
Show help and exit.
.br
Applies to: every command. Config key: help. Env: SR_HELP.
.TP
.B \-\-help\-all
Show help for every subcommand and option.
.br
Applies to: every command. Config key: help_all. Env: SR_HELP_ALL.
.TP
.B \-V, \-\-version
Print version, installation and update information.
.br
Applies to: every command. Config key: version. Env: SR_VERSION.
.TP
.B \-\-debug
Debug logging (same as \-vv).
.br
Applies to: every command. Config key: debug. Env: SR_DEBUG.
.TP
.B \-\-no\-update\-check
Do not check for a newer release in the background.
.br
Applies to: every command. Config key: no_update_check. Env: SR_NO_UPDATE_CHECK.
.TP
.B \-\-config KEY=VALUE
Override one configuration value (repeatable).
.br
Applies to: every command. Config key: config. Env: SR_CONFIG.
.TP
.B \-\-config\-file FILE
Read an extra configuration file (repeatable).
.br
Applies to: every command. Config key: config_file. Env: SR_CONFIG_FILE.
.TP
.B \-v, \-\-verbose
Show per\-file results and the reason skipped files were skipped.
.br
Applies to: every command. Config key: verbose. Env: SR_VERBOSE.
.TP
.B \-q, \-\-quiet
Only errors; suitable for scripts.
.br
Applies to: every command. Config key: quiet. Env: SR_QUIET.
.TP
.B \-\-verbosity LEVEL
Set the log level explicitly (overrides \-v/\-q). One of: quiet, normal, verbose, debug. Default: normal.
.br
Applies to: every command. Config key: verbosity. Env: SR_VERBOSITY.
.TP
.B \-\-color, \-\-no\-color WHEN
Colourise output. Honours NO_COLOR and CLICOLOR_FORCE. One of: auto, always, never. Default: auto.
.br
Applies to: every command. Config key: color. Env: SR_COLOR.
.TP
.B \-\-json
Emit a single JSON document on stdout; diagnostics go to stderr.
.br
Applies to: every command. Config key: json. Env: SR_JSON.
.TP
.B \-\-porcelain
Emit stable \(aqpath:line:col:text\(aq records, one per match.
.br
Applies to: replace, find, diff, rename. Config key: porcelain. Env: SR_PORCELAIN.
.TP
.B \-\-progress, \-\-no\-progress
Show a progress bar on a terminal (default on). Default: True.
.br
Applies to: replace, find, diff, rename. Config key: progress. Env: SR_PROGRESS.
.TP
.B \-\-summary, \-\-no\-summary
Print the run summary (default on). Default: True.
.br
Applies to: replace, find, diff, rename. Config key: summary. Env: SR_SUMMARY.
.SS replace only
.TP
.B \-\-glob GLOB
File name pattern (repeatable); alternative to the positional GLOB.
.SS find only
.TP
.B \-\-glob GLOB
File name pattern (repeatable).
.TP
.B \-c, \-\-count
Print only the number of matches per file (and a total).
.TP
.B \-l, \-\-files\-with\-matches
Print only the names of files that contain a match.
.TP
.B \-A, \-\-after\-context N
Print N lines after each match.
.TP
.B \-B, \-\-before\-context N
Print N lines before each match.
.TP
.B \-\-highlight
Force match highlighting even when writing to a file.
.SS diff only
.TP
.B \-\-session SESSION
Instead of computing a diff, show the diff stored in a session.
.SS rename only
.TP
.B \-\-directories
Also rename directories that match (default: files only).
.SS rollback only
.TP
.B \-\-no\-safety\-snapshot
Do not record the pre\-rollback state (not recommended).
.SS sessions only
.TP
.B \-\-limit N
List at most N sessions (default 20).
.TP
.B \-\-files
Include the per\-file list in \(gashow\(ga.
.SS update only
.TP
.B \-\-check
Only report whether an update exists.
.TP
.B \-\-channel
Release channel (default: stable).
.TP
.B \-\-source
Where to fetch release information (default: github).
.TP
.B \-\-repo OWNER/NAME
Override the GitHub repository.
.TP
.B \-\-timeout SECONDS
Network timeout (default 20).
.TP
.B \-\-path FILE
Standalone file to update (default: auto\-detect).
.TP
.B \-\-no\-verify
Install without SHA\-256 verification. Verification is fail\-closed by default, so this is the only way past a missing checksum — use it only for a mirror you trust.
.SS doctor only
.TP
.B \-\-offline
Skip the network reachability probe.
.SS completions only
.TP
.B \-\-install
Write the script into the shell\(aqs completion directory.
.SS config only
.TP
.B \-\-option NAME
Option name for \(gashow\(ga, e.g. \(gasr config show \-\-option max\-size\(ga.
.TP
.B \-\-show\-origin
Show where each value comes from.
.SS selftest only
.TP
.B \-\-quick
Run only the essential checks.
.SS help only
.TP
.B \-\-option NAME
Explain one option, e.g. \(gasr help \-\-option max\-count\(ga.
.TP
.B \-\-all
Same as \(gasr \-\-help\-all\(ga.
.TP
.B \-\-no\-pager
Never page the output.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
success (for replace/find: at least one match was found)
.TP
.B 1
runtime error (I/O failure, corrupt session, internal bug)
.TP
.B 2
usage error (bad arguments, bad configuration file)
.TP
.B 3
no match: the pattern was not found in any target file
.TP
.B 4
rollback failed for one or more files
.TP
.B 5
self\-update failed (network, checksum, permissions)
.TP
.B 6
selftest failed: installation is broken
.TP
.B 130
interrupted by the user (SIGINT)
.SH ENVIRONMENT
Every option has an SR_* variable: the option name in upper case with
'-' replaced by '_'. A variable overrides configuration files but is
overridden by the command line.
.TP
.B SR_TARGET
Same as \-\-target. Directory to operate on (default: the current directory).
.TP
.B SR_PATTERN
Same as \-\-pattern. Only files whose name matches GLOB (repeatable). Default: every text file.
.TP
.B SR_FILE
Same as \-\-file. Explicit file to process, bypassing pattern matching (repeatable).
.TP
.B SR_FILES_FROM
Same as \-\-files\-from. Read the list of target files from FILE, one per line (\(aq\-\(aq for stdin).
.TP
.B SR_INCLUDE
Same as \-\-include. Additional include glob, may contain \(aq/\(aq and \(aq**\(aq (repeatable).
.TP
.B SR_EXCLUDE
Same as \-\-exclude. Exclude glob; wins over \-\-include and \-\-pattern (repeatable).
.TP
.B SR_EXCLUDE_DIR
Same as \-\-exclude\-dir. Prune a directory by name (repeatable; adds to the built\-in list).
.TP
.B SR_NO_DEFAULT_EXCLUDES
Same as \-\-no\-default\-excludes. Do not apply the built\-in exclude lists (node_modules, dist, *.bak …).
.TP
.B SR_NO_RECURSIVE
Same as \-\-no\-recursive. Only the target directory itself, no subdirectories.
.TP
.B SR_MAX_DEPTH
Same as \-\-max\-depth. Descend at most N levels below the target directory.
.TP
.B SR_HIDDEN
Same as \-\-hidden. Also process dotfiles and dot\-directories.
.TP
.B SR_SYMLINKS
Same as \-\-symlinks. Follow symlinked files (they are skipped by default).
.TP
.B SR_GITIGNORE
Same as \-\-gitignore. Honour .gitignore files (including the global excludesfile).
.TP
.B SR_MAX_SIZE
Same as \-\-max\-size. Skip files larger than SIZE (default 100MB; a bare number means megabytes).
.TP
.B SR_MIN_SIZE
Same as \-\-min\-size. Skip files smaller than SIZE.
.TP
.B SR_NEWER_THAN
Same as \-\-newer\-than. Only files modified within AGE (e.g. 24h, 7d, 30m).
.TP
.B SR_OLDER_THAN
Same as \-\-older\-than. Only files not modified within AGE.
.TP
.B SR_BINARY
Same as \-\-binary. Process files that look binary instead of skipping them.
.TP
.B SR_REGEX
Same as \-\-regex. Treat PATTERN as a Python regular expression.
.TP
.B SR_ERE
Same as \-\-ere. Treat PATTERN as a POSIX Extended Regular Expression (grep \-E syntax).
.TP
.B SR_PCRE
Same as \-\-pcre. Treat PATTERN as a Perl\-compatible regular expression.
.TP
.B SR_PATTERN_KIND
Same as \-\-pattern\-kind. Explicit pattern kind: fixed, regex, ere or pcre.
.TP
.B SR_IGNORE_CASE
Same as \-\-ignore\-case. Case\-insensitive matching.
.TP
.B SR_WORD_BOUNDARY
Same as \-\-word\-boundary. Match whole words only.
.TP
.B SR_MULTILINE
Same as \-\-multiline. Allow the pattern to span line boundaries (\(aq.\(aq matches newline).
.TP
.B SR_NO_ESCAPES
Same as \-\-no\-escapes. Do not interpret \(rsn, \(rst, \(rsxNN in fixed patterns/replacements.
.TP
.B SR_LINE_RANGE
Same as \-\-line\-range. Restrict edits to a line range: 10, 10\-20, 10\- or \-20.
.TP
.B SR_MAX_COUNT
Same as \-\-max\-count. Replace at most N occurrences per file.
.TP
.B SR_NO_GLOBAL
Same as \-\-no\-global. Replace only the first occurrence on each line.
.TP
.B SR_CONTEXT
Same as \-\-context. Lines of context around a match in preview/diff output (default 3).
.TP
.B SR_DRY_RUN
Same as \-\-dry\-run. Report what would change; never write, never create a session.
.TP
.B SR_DIFF
Same as \-\-diff. Show a unified diff of every change (implies a preview).
.TP
.B SR_INTERACTIVE
Same as \-\-interactive. Confirm every hunk before it is written.
.TP
.B SR_WRITE
Same as \-\-write. Where the result goes: in\-place (default), stdout, or \-\-output\-dir.
.TP
.B SR_OUTPUT_DIR
Same as \-\-output\-dir. Write modified copies under DIR, mirroring the tree (needs \-\-write=dir).
.TP
.B SR_SUFFIX
Same as \-\-suffix. When writing copies, append EXT to the file name instead of mirroring.
.TP
.B SR_RENAME
Same as \-\-rename. Also apply the replacement to file and directory names.
.TP
.B SR_RENAME_ONLY
Same as \-\-rename\-only. Rename matching files/directories without touching their content.
.TP
.B SR_ENCODING
Same as \-\-encoding. Force an encoding (utf\-8, cp1251, latin\-1 …); default is auto\-detect.
.TP
.B SR_ENCODING_ERROR
Same as \-\-encoding\-error. What to do with undecodable bytes: strict (skip), replace, ignore.
.TP
.B SR_LINE_ENDINGS
Same as \-\-line\-endings. Keep the file\(aqs line endings (default) or normalise to LF/CRLF.
.TP
.B SR_PRESERVE_MTIME
Same as \-\-preserve\-mtime. Restore the original modification time after writing.
.TP
.B SR_BACKUP
Same as \-\-backup. Store originals in a session so the run can be rolled back (default on).
.TP
.B SR_BACKUP_DIR
Same as \-\-backup\-dir. Where to keep sessions (default <target>/.sr). Use an absolute path to keep them out of the tree.
.TP
.B SR_KEEP
Same as \-\-keep. Keep at most N sessions; older ones are pruned after a run (default 20, 0 disables pruning).
.TP
.B SR_KEEP_AGE
Same as \-\-keep\-age. Also drop sessions older than AGE (e.g. 30d).
.TP
.B SR_ATOMIC
Same as \-\-atomic. Write via a temporary file + rename (default) or rewrite the inode in place.
.TP
.B SR_LOCK
Same as \-\-lock. Take an advisory lock so two sr runs cannot interleave (default on).
.TP
.B SR_CONFIRM_THRESHOLD
Same as \-\-confirm\-threshold. Ask for confirmation when more than N files would change (0 disables).
.TP
.B SR_ALLOW_ROOT
Same as \-\-allow\-root. Permit a mutating run with euid 0 (refused by default).
.TP
.B SR_FORCE
Same as \-\-force. Override safety guards (drifted files, read\-only warnings, existing targets).
.TP
.B SR_VERBOSE
Same as \-\-verbose. Show per\-file results and the reason skipped files were skipped.
.TP
.B SR_QUIET
Same as \-\-quiet. Only errors; suitable for scripts.
.TP
.B SR_VERBOSITY
Same as \-\-verbosity. Set the log level explicitly (overrides \-v/\-q).
.TP
.B SR_COLOR
Same as \-\-color. Colourise output. Honours NO_COLOR and CLICOLOR_FORCE.
.TP
.B SR_JSON
Same as \-\-json. Emit a single JSON document on stdout; diagnostics go to stderr.
.TP
.B SR_YES
Same as \-\-yes. Answer \(aqyes\(aq to every prompt; required for unattended runs in CI.
.TP
.B SR_PORCELAIN
Same as \-\-porcelain. Emit stable \(aqpath:line:col:text\(aq records, one per match.
.TP
.B SR_PROGRESS
Same as \-\-progress. Show a progress bar on a terminal (default on).
.TP
.B SR_SUMMARY
Same as \-\-summary. Print the run summary (default on).
.TP
.B SR_NO_UPDATE_CHECK
Set to 1 to disable the background update check.
.TP
.B SR_UPDATE_REPO
Override the GitHub repository used by \(gasr update\(ga.
.TP
.B SR_GITHUB_TOKEN
Bearer token for the GitHub API (raises the rate limit).
.TP
.B SR_CONFIG
Path to an extra configuration file.
.TP
.B SR_DEBUG
Set to 1 to print a traceback when sr crashes.
.TP
.B SR_OFFLINE
Set to 1 to skip the network probe in \(gasr doctor\(ga.
.TP
.B SR_COMPLETION_SHELL
Force the shell for \(gasr completions\(ga.
.TP
.B NO_COLOR
Disable colours (https://no\-color.org).
.TP
.B CLICOLOR_FORCE
Force colours even when stdout is not a terminal.
.TP
.B COLUMNS
Terminal width used for wrapping help output.
.SH FILES
.TP
.B <target>/.sr/sessions/<id>/
Session store: manifest.json, journal.jsonl, a content-addressed blob
store under files/, and per-file diffs. Created by every mutating run.
Add .sr/ to .gitignore.
.TP
.B /etc/sr/sr.conf
System-wide configuration (lowest priority).
.TP
.B $XDG_CONFIG_HOME/sr/sr.conf
User configuration; ~/.config/sr/sr.conf by default.
.TP
.B <project>/.srrc
Project configuration, found by walking up to the repository root.
.TP
.B $XDG_CACHE_HOME/sr/update-check.json
Cache for the background update check (24h TTL).
